00:46:46.000 --> 00:47:22.000
The more we rush, the easier it becomes to trip over something and take a fall. Checklists won't necessarily solve all problems of modern living. Airplanes and space shuttles will still crash, despite elaborate checklists. Surgeries will still go wrong, even when a team takes the time to anticipate the risks. Merely setting up checklists won't mean that we follow them. We can set up a calendar alert, reminding us of a 5pm project deadline, and then procrastinate all the way until 445 before we try to get it done. Checklists can also be so long and complicated that they become self-defeating.
我们越是匆忙，就越容易被什么东西绊倒并摔倒。核对清单不一定能解决现代生活中的所有问题。尽管有精心制作的核对清单，飞机和航天飞机仍然会坠毁。即使团队花时间去预见风险，手术仍然会出错。仅仅建立核对清单并不意味着我们会遵循它们。我们可以设置日历提醒，提醒我们下午5点有一个项目截止日期，然后一直拖延到4点45分才试图去完成它。核对清单也可能又长又复杂，以至于适得其反。

86
00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:42.000
You can draw up a comprehensive list before heading to the supermarket and then come home and find you forgot to get the coffee. But what checklists do accomplish is they get us to focus on things that are most likely to trip us up. Over time, preventing the most common errors will have the biggest impact on our lives.
你可以在去超市前拟定一份详尽的清单，结果回家后发现自己忘了买咖啡。但核对清单真正做到的是，它们让我们专注于最容易绊倒我们的事情。随着时间的推移，预防最常见的错误将对我们的生活产生最大的影响。

